{"causes": ["The long-standing 'Auld Alliance' between Scotland and France provided the institutional framework for Scottish nobles like Stewart to serve the French crown.", "The ongoing dynastic ambitions of the French Valois kings, particularly Charles VIII and Louis XII, necessitated skilled military commanders and diplomats for their Italian campaigns.", "The Stewart of Darnley family's established connections to French aristocracy offered Bernard a direct path into high-ranking military and diplomatic service."], "effects": ["Bernard Stewart's victories in the Italian Wars temporarily solidified French control over parts of Southern Italy, influencing the geopolitical map of the peninsula.", "His leadership reinforced the historical role and prestige of the Garde Écossaise, integrating Scottish military tradition deeply into the French armed forces.", "His diplomatic efforts helped to sustain the Franco-Scottish alliance, which continued to be a significant factor in European power politics throughout the 16th century."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "charles-viii-of-france", "sourceName": "Charles VIII of France", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny", "targetName": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "context": "Bernard Stewart served as a key military commander for King Charles VIII during the First Italian War, notably at the Battle of Seminara in 1495."}, {"sourceSlug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny", "sourceName": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "italian-wars", "targetName": "Italian Wars", "context": "Bernard Stewart was a prominent French commander throughout the Italian Wars, leading forces in Naples and Calabria between 1495 and 1503."}, {"sourceSlug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny", "sourceName": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "kingdom-of-naples", "targetName": "Kingdom of Naples", "context": "Bernard Stewart served as Lieutenant-General of the French army in the Kingdom of Naples during the Second Italian War, commanding the siege of Capua in 1501."}, {"sourceSlug": "garde-ecossaise", "sourceName": "Garde Écossaise", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny", "targetName": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "context": "Bernard Stewart held the distinguished position of Captain of the Garde Écossaise, the personal bodyguard of the French king, for many years."}, {"sourceSlug": "auld-alliance", "sourceName": "Auld Alliance", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny", "targetName": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "context": "The historical 'Auld Alliance' between Scotland and France provided the foundational context for Bernard Stewart's career in French military and diplomatic service."}], "places": [{"name": "Naples, Italy", "role": "Military Command"}, {"name": "Seminara, Italy", "role": "Battle Site"}, {"name": "Edinburgh, Scotland", "role": "Diplomatic Mission"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bernard Stewart, 3rd Lord of Aubigny", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bernard-stewart-3rd-lord-of-aubigny"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2747051", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Stewart,_3rd_Lord_of_Aubigny"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bernard Stewart's military leadership in the Italian Wars significantly influenced the temporary French occupation of Southern Italy and reinforced the enduring Franco-Scottish alliance. His career exemplifies the critical role of foreign mercenaries and diplomats in shaping Renaissance European power dynamics.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-29T05:16:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33235638494", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Seigneur d'Aubigny was a French soldier, commander of the Garde Écossaise, and diplomat belonging to the Scottish family of Stewart of Darnley.", "newValue": "Bernard Stewart, 3rd Seigneur d'Aubigny, a scion of the Scottish House of Stewart of Darnley, emerged as a pivotal military commander and diplomat for the French crown during the late 15th and early 16th centuries. Born into a family with deep ties to both Scotland and France, his career epitomized the 'Auld Alliance' and the intricate dynastic politics of the era. Serving under King Charles VIII and Louis XII, Aubigny commanded the elite Garde Écossaise, a testament to the enduring military bond between the two nations, and played a crucial role in France's Italian Wars, shaping the geopolitical landscape of Renaissance Europe.

Aubigny's military prowess was demonstrated most notably during the Italian Wars. He commanded French forces at the Battle of Seminara in 1495, decisively defeating Spanish and Neapolitan armies and securing French control over Calabria for a period. His strategic acumen was further highlighted during the Second Italian War (1499-1504), where he served as Lieutenant-General of the French army in Naples, overseeing the siege of Capua in 1501 and the subsequent occupation of the Kingdom of Naples. Beyond the battlefield, Aubigny undertook critical diplomatic missions, including negotiating with King James IV of Scotland to renew the Franco-Scottish alliance in 1508, shortly before his death.

His legacy is multifaceted, reflecting both military achievement and diplomatic influence. Aubigny's campaigns in Italy, though ultimately unsuccessful in securing permanent French dominance, profoundly impacted the balance of power on the peninsula, contributing to the protracted conflicts that would define Italian politics for decades. His leadership of the Garde Écossaise reinforced the institutionalized presence of Scottish military service in France, a tradition that persisted for centuries. He also served as a cultural bridge, embodying the fusion of Scottish heritage and French courtly and military life."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-29T05:16:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33235638494", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1452"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1452"}], "newValue": ["The long-standing 'Auld Alliance' between Scotland and France provided the institutional framework for Scottish nobles like Stewart to serve the French crown.", "The ongoing dynastic ambitions of the French Valois kings, particularly Charles VIII and Louis XII, necessitated skilled military commanders and diplomats for their Italian campaigns.", "The Stewart of Darnley family's established connections to French aristocracy offered Bernard a direct path into high-ranking military and diplomatic service."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-29T05:16:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33235638494", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1508-06-15"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1508-06-15"}], "newValue": ["Bernard Stewart's victories in the Italian Wars 